<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/expert/earth-system-tipping-points">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Tipping points are <strong>the non-linear edge of climate science</strong> — thresholds past
    which change becomes self-driving and irreversible. Their probability this century is
    uncertain; their consequences are permanent. That combination, not headline temperatures,
    is what "avoiding dangerous interference" actually means.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the definition, the candidates, the cascades, the honest uncertainty" widget="equationStep" config={tipCfg} />

  <h2>How the field studies them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Paleoclimate precedent (the ice-age lesson):</strong> Dansgaard–Oeschger events prove the coupled system CAN jump — the question is which thresholds lie ahead, not whether jumping exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Early-warning signals:</strong> systems approaching bifurcation show "critical slowing down" — recovery from fluctuations lengthens. Detected in some real systems (lakes, possibly AMOC proxies), an active research frontier.</li>
    <li><strong>Runaway greenhouse (Venus) is NOT on the table:</strong> Earth cannot replicate Venus this century (the planetary lesson\'s comparison) — confusing near-certain bad with apocalyptic fantasy hurts the argument\'s credibility, which is why scientists say it plainly.</li>
    <li><strong>Governance of thresholds is unsolved:</strong> the Paris temperature goals are partly a tipping-point hedge; some researchers discuss solar geoengineering as an emergency brake — a governance question (the global-governance and AI-alignment lessons\' domain) more than a scientific one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lenton et al., PNAS (2008); Armstrong McKay et al., Science (2022); Global Tipping Points Report (2023); IPCC AR6 on abrupt change.</p>
</TopicPage>
